# netsurf: a class change on a table cell's own box rebuilds its table alone (patch 369)
# Generated 2026-11-01
# Base: seleron/vitabrowser (patch stack through 368)
#
# The last WS-C script change on the full rebuild: a class change on a
# <td>'s own box ("class change on an element with a non-inline,
# non-block box"), 93-98 ms on a 2500-paragraph sim page. A cell's box
# carries what the table works out from every cell -- its start column
# and, in border-collapse, its used borders from its neighbours (table
# normalisation, table.c) -- and the column types come from every cell's
# style. So the scope is the table.
#
#  - html.c: html_rl_table_restyle() takes the cell's table box out (a
#    real <table> whose box sits in a block or a cell), cascades the
#    table's subtree afresh, builds the <table> element again, puts its
#    box back where the old one was, normalises it as a full build does,
#    and lays the page out once -- which makes the column types again
#    (layout.c). Declined: a table holding objects, controls, frames or
#    list markers; a page whose CSS mentions counters (a class could
#    change one read outside the table). :has() is not a reach out: this
#    libcss parses it and never matches it (patch 328). Logged "table
#    restyled, full layout (a cell's own class)".
#  - box_normalise.c/h: box_normalise_table_ext(), the static
#    box_normalise_table for one table.
#
# Result: 94 -> 17 ms. Nine steps (a width class, a thick collapsed
# border, a header's font, a colspan cell's padding, a class removed, a
# nested table's cell, a class a td.hl + td selector reads, ::before, a
# border-spacing table) render row for row as a parse-time build of the
# same DOM at every step; :has(), counter and image variants likewise.
#
